From 8d80a35dde77a7e184c4c62eea9c714ebfa1e3fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:27:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] microblaze: add SET_ULEB128 and SUB_ULEB128 relocations

gas resolves a .uleb128 of a difference of code labels at assembly
time, even when an IMM between the labels can later be deleted by
linker relaxation.  GCC writes location and range lists this way
(".uleb128 .LVL2-1-.Ltext0"), and so does its line table when gas does
not generate it.  In the new test, a difference of 132 stays 132 after
relaxation shrinks it to 124.

Add R_MICROBLAZE_SET_ULEB128 and R_MICROBLAZE_SUB_ULEB128, after
R_RISCV_SET_ULEB128 and R_RISCV_SUB_ULEB128.  gas gives each such
.uleb128 the pair, both at the field, in microblaze_pre_output_hook,
as riscv_insert_uleb128_fixes does, and only when both labels are in
one code section and the difference was not resolved when it was
parsed.  It still writes the difference it computed, so an unrelaxed
link and tools reading the object see the right value.
microblaze_elf_relocate_section rewrites the field from the pair in
the number of bytes it already occupies, padding with continuation
bytes; relaxation only deletes bytes, so the new value always fits.
Relaxation adjusts the addends the same way as for the other ADD and
SUB relocations, and readelf applies the pair when dumping an object.

The numbers 44 and 45 follow the provisional block 36-43 used for the
ADD, SUB and SET relocations, and need the same agreement with AMD.

